Subject: Soaking the hapless taxpayers

    After reading Mark's message, I'm reminded of a sort of prisoner's
dilemma between the taker and the rest of society. Imagine this
payoff matrix.

                           Taker
     Accept Govt Money                  Decline

 A    everyone suffers                  taker suffer


 D    taker benefits, society suffers   government irrelevent


   Everyone declining to accept government pay outs would result in
less taxes and everyone being able to keep their money. So (D,D) is
what we are shooting for. But by defecting and acceptng an SBIR or
whatever, you can benefit greatly while the suckers (taxpayers) bear
the burden. Of course, everyone else realizes this and we arrive at
(A,A) where everyone gets the sucker's payoff.

  However, the iterated dilemma can't be applied like the normal
IPD because you never meet or personally interact with the other
players. How do you play tit-for-tat when you don't know who is
defecting and who isn't? It seems hopeless. The current practice
seems to be sort of a game between large lobbying groups
(I'll cut my taxpayer pet project if you cut yours, or I'll vote 
or yours if you vote for mine) This doesn't work at all for the people
who own the money that is being spent -- the people who need information
in order to make an informed vote. Most people who vote don't even
know or care about how individual representatives performed -- whether
your district got a sucker's payoff or someone else's did. What does
game theory say about the efficiency of voting vs markets?




