Date:     Wed, 5 Jan 94 13:04:29 EST
From: cards@top.cis.syr.edu
Subject: Soaking the hapless taxpayers

MY personal rules on taking government money follow...

1) Never lobby the government to increase total spending.

2) If you must lobby the government to increase spending
on a particular program, push it on the basis of reallocating
money from bad programs to good ones.

3) Realize that if you attempt (2) above, and are at all
succesful in getting the spending increase on the program
you like, you will almost certainly have indirectly violated
rule (1) above, because the government is much more likely
to increase total spending to support your program than to
reallocate money from bad programs.

4) Never accept social welfare unless it is that or starve,
and even then do so only as a very temporary emergency
measure.

5) Realize that the arguments about 'getting back' some of
what you have had taken are specious: the government did not
save what it took from you -- it spent it -- therefore you
are not getting back what was stolen, you are promoting the
stealing of more.

6) Realize on the other hand that if the government is
committed to spending a particular dollar, it is better
that they spend it on you than on someone else.  So take
all you can get of already-allocated monies.

7) Don't forget that following rule (6) above leads to
expansion of the program in most cases, indirectly
involving rule (5) above.

8) If you must take money to do something, try to make it
something which really will give the poor taxpayers a
return on their involuntary investment.  For instance,
don't be a 'Beltway Bandit' if you are a contractor;
instead generate new intellectual property which will
generate wealth (net) for the country as a whole, vs
simply redistributing some of it into your pocket.

9) Get the hell off the dole ASAP.

I am currently starting work on a Small Business Innovation
Research Phase II contract with the Advanced Research
Projects Agency (through the US Army Missile Command at
Redstone Arsenal).  I MAY seek other government contract
work for a little while (or I may not); but I will not
take on any new contracts whose period of performance will
extend beyond the end of the current effort.  When this
effort is done, I am quit of government contracting.
I don't feel good about taking this money, but I will
put myself in a much better position by doing so.  Once
I am personally and corporately (is that a word?) solvent
again, I will restrict my future activities to the private
sector, and specifically to the creation of new technology
(and application of technology) in pursuit of explicitly
extropian / libertian / anarchocapitalist / etc. ends.
I do not mean to attempt to restrict how my output is
used by the buyers, but I do intend to produce output
which is inherently more useful to and/or more likely to
be effectively utilized by individuals than by coercive
organizations.  Basically I make tools which can be
used by individuals to enhance themselves, their abilities,
their lives and especially their liberties.  Unfortunately
at the moment I am taking money from the government to do it;
that will stop no later than 30 JUN 1996, and the earlier
the better.
