nanotech...in some ways it's much _more_ important, as cyberspace is
much more tangible in a weird way than mundane space, and in many
other ways it's certainly _less_ revolutionary. Plenty of room at the
fringes, to coin a phrase.)

The surge in interest in crypto is amazing. But few profits lie there,
for the near term. Sometimes I think most of the people around me are
trying to figure an angle on this...which is good, but scary. Scary in
that I can't see how they'll make much money in the next couple of
years on this.

For instance, you've all heard the talk about digital banks...this
came up in the thread Bill Garland just started, with his plans. And
there are plans in the Austin Cypherpunks group for such a thing, and
something major brewing in the California Cypherpunks group, about
which I'll say little more (except that I'm _not_ involved). A couple
of folks back East are also talking along these lines.

And at a New Year's Eve Party, some members of this List, who can
identify themselves if they wish (of course) asked my what I thought
were the hot areas of crypto that some budding entrepreneurs could get
into...or at least this was my understanding of their question
(parties are pretty bad places to get real communication, so I may
have misunderstood their interests). I shrugged and told them that
even Phil Zimmermann, creator of the most significant product in
years, PGP, is unlikely to make a dime, net, off his work. Thus, I can
see few immediate opportunities for profit-making.

More opportunities than in nanotech, but still damn few. (The likeliest
big profiter from the current interest in crypto is Bruce Schneier,
the auhtor of a new and hot-selling book on crypto....proving that, as
with nanotech, the real profits are in writing the books!)

This will change, as more and more commerce shifts into cypberspace.
Crypto is the only "glue" and "nails" and "mortar" that can hold up
the walls and structures of cyberspace, that can "keep the roads open"
and that can create defensive barriers and colonizable spaces. Thus,
various forms of crypto--encryption, digital money, untraceable
routing, anonymous voting, reputation databases, zero knowledge--will
be the preeminent technologies in use in cyberspace.

But most folks today don't think they need crypto, and those that do
bellyache about the $125 that the crypto vendor RSA Data Security Inc.
charges! Hence their enthusiasm for the "free" software PGP, for which
Phil Zimmermann and the others who volunteered time have received but
a pittance. It's hard to make money in the "straight encryption"
market (which is overcrowded....look at the various poorly-selling
encryption packages for the Mac, for example).

The real money will be made in new areas.

Looking out 5-15 years, some folks--perhaps some of you--will make
millions on crypto/cyberspace-related products. Maybe a MOO/MUD a la
Habitat that offers "True Names"-like interactivity (and hence
economic interactions). Maybe an EDI-based market system, sort of an
"AMIX II." Maybe a voice encryption box that sells like hotcakes in
Fry's. Maybe a digital bank that by 2010 has most of Bill Gates' money
in it. 

But don't ask me what the obvious short-term investments are, or where
you should concentrate your efforts. I'll gladly shoot the breeze on
this topic for hours and hours, but I have no simple answers.

I'm expecting the Crypto Phase Change, the Crypto Rapture, but I'm not
holding my breath (a strange mixed diving metaphor, eh?).

--Tim May


