From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Crypto Opportunities

Tim May writes some interesting comments on crypto-business.
An important point here is that very few people in the
Extropians/cypherpunks community have extensive experience in
banking, billing, headhunting, gambling, or the black market,
which are the core areas of competence needed to succeed in the
largest markets.  Crypto technology is a vital component
for enabling jurisdictional independence, but it is cheap and
will be available to any clued-in competitor who chooses to
deploy it.  Just as vital are wide-area network software
(making unreliable connections reliable) and user-freindly client
software.  But these enablers are not core areas of competence for 
operating the businesses or assessing their viability.

Thus, few of our opinions on the near-term viability of crypto-business
are well-founded.  My own lay opinion, based on broad-based study
of these areas, is that $trillions worth of potential markets exist for 
net-based banking, crypto-commuting, etc. and that the barrier to 
crypto-business is not time or the need for technology to mature,
but cultural gap between traditional business protocols on the one 
hand, and complex technology like crypto protocols on the other hand.  
A minor but revealing symptom: does Eric Hughes _look_ like a 
banker? :-)

This mismatch of skill and interest is similar to the mismatch 
between our great interest in life extension and lack of expertise 
in medicine.  In both cases the mismatch is a grave handicap to 
our efforts.  Learning these areas is a long haul, made all the
harder by our lack of contacts in these areas.  Focused 
outreach and team-building in the areas of banking and medicine 
are vital, IMHO.

